From: zirize <zirize@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 04:58:13 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 0/5] ui: fix relative pointer motion, and add a scaler option for SDL

The first four patches are fixes for things I ran into running a Mac OS 9
guest (relative pointer only, no absolute tablet) on a KDE/Wayland host.
The last one adds a feature that depends on the third.

Patches 1 and 4 are the same bug in two places. Both frontends scale the
relative motion down to guest coordinates and truncate the result to an
integer, so the fraction is dropped on every event instead of being
accumulated. Whenever the window is larger than the guest the factor is
below one, and a slow movement of the mouse produces a delta of zero over
and over: the guest pointer does not move at all until the mouse is moved
fast enough, which reads as a broken acceleration curve. Over a longer
movement the discarded fractions add up and the pointer falls short of
where it should be. Both patches keep the remainder and fold it into the
next event.

Patch 2 stops ui/gtk from recording itself as the pointer owner when
gdk_seat_grab() has failed. The return value is currently discarded and
ptr_owner is set unconditionally, so a failed grab leaves QEMU feeding
relative motion to the guest while the host pointer is free to wander
over other windows, with nothing to indicate why the two cursors have
come apart.

Patch 3 asks SDL for the output's real pixel density. Without
SDL_WINDOW_ALLOW_HIGHDPI the window is only drawn at its logical size and
a compositor at a fractional scale resamples it with its own filter, so
the guest comes out bilinear-blurred whatever filtering QEMU picked. On a
1.45x display here the difference is plain: measured against the guest
framebuffer, the window content matches a bilinear upscale before the
patch and a nearest one after it.

Patch 5 then adds scaler=linear|nearest|integer|integerplus to the SDL
display. nearest and linear pick the filter; integer scales by a whole
number and letterboxes the rest so no guest pixel is wider than another;
integerplus scales by a whole number into a render target and stretches
that to the window, filling it without the uneven pixels of plain nearest
neighbour.

integer and integerplus allow the window to fall a couple of pixels short
of a whole multiple before giving up on it. That is not slack for its own
sake: a fractional-scale compositor rounds the surface to whole physical
pixels, so a window sized to be exactly N times the guest can come back a
pixel short, and taking the floor there drops a whole step - 2x becomes
1x and half the window turns into border. Within that tolerance
integerplus also skips its second pass entirely, since resampling by one
pixel is all blur and no gain.

The option is on DisplayOptions rather than DisplaySDL so the other
backends can implement it too - ui/gtk would be the obvious next one.

Tested on Ubuntu 26.04, KDE Plasma on Wayland at a 1.45 scale, SDL
2.32.10, with a mac99 Mac OS 9.2.2 guest and a q35 Windows guest.

zirize (5):
  ui/gtk: keep the fraction when scaling relative motion
  ui/gtk: do not take ownership of the pointer if the grab failed
  ui/sdl: render at the output's pixel density
  ui/sdl: keep the fraction when scaling relative motion
  ui: add a scaler option for the SDL display

